From: Jerome Berclaz <26882162+jberclaz@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: tegra: mask imprecise aborts before disabling the data cache

On the Microsoft Surface RT (Tegra 30, Cortex-A9 r2p9) booted through
U-Boot, taking a CPU offline hangs the system, and so does every
suspend, since suspend takes the secondary CPUs offline first.

Step markers written by the dying CPU to a PMC scratch register show it
stopping inside tegra_disable_clean_inv_dcache(), right after SCTLR.C has
been cleared and the CPU has left SMP coherency, at a different point in
each build. Removing the PL310 from the devicetree does not change this.
Masking imprecise aborts and FIQs before the cache is disabled lets the
CPU get through the flush (ISR.A is clear when the mask is set, so the
abort arrives during the cache shutdown, and taking it in that state
wedges the CPU).

tegra30_cpu_shutdown() already masks imprecise aborts, but only after the
cache has been disabled. Mask them, and FIQs, at the start of
tegra_disable_clean_inv_dcache(), which all the power-down paths (hotplug,
LP2 cpuidle, suspend) go through. They are not re-enabled on the way down,
and __tegra_cpu_reset_handler masks them again on wake-up.

On the Surface RT this is needed together with "ARM: tegra: use a direct
call to power off a dying CPU". Tested with both on kernel 7.3.1
(libre-tegra eab1ff1e), U-Boot RAM boot: 10 rounds of taking cpu1-3
offline and online, and 5 consecutive "deep" (LP2) suspend/resume cycles,
with cpuidle off and with cpuidle (C7/CC6) enabled.

Assisted-by: Claude (Anthropic)
---
--- a/arch/arm/mach-tegra/sleep.S
+++ b/arch/arm/mach-tegra/sleep.S
@@ -33,6 +33,16 @@
  * Corrupted registers: r1-r3, r6, r8, r9-r11
  */
 ENTRY(tegra_disable_clean_inv_dcache)
+	/*
+	 * Mask imprecise aborts and FIQs before the D-cache is disabled.
+	 * Once the cache is off and the CPU has left coherency, taking an
+	 * exception here hangs the CPU. tegra30_cpu_shutdown() masks aborts
+	 * only after this function has run; on the Microsoft Surface RT an
+	 * imprecise abort arrives in between, hanging CPU hotplug, suspend
+	 * and power-gated cpuidle. Nothing re-enables them on the way down,
+	 * and the reset handler masks them again on wake-up.
+	 */
+	cpsid	af
 	stmfd	sp!, {r0, r4-r5, r7, r9-r11, lr}
 	dmb					@ ensure ordering
 
